[{:db/id -1 :doc/id "business-openrouter-modal-backend-qualification-20260908" :doc/title "Modal-backed Murakumo qualification" :doc/as-of "2026-09-08" :doc/status :draft :source/dataset "business" :doc/body "# Modal-backed Murakumo: qualification and commercial evidence\n\nOwner direction: Modal is the backend; keep Slow, but cheap. Status: preparation advanced, not qualified or submitted. The earlier b70 results remain historical evidence for that exact backend only.\n\n## Runtime identity\nAt API main 969928a, Basho routes Hugging Face, Modal Shared, then OpenRouter. Existing Modal Shared URL is https://junkawasakicom--ep-glm-5-3-flash-server.us-west.modal.direct/v1/chat/completions for zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash. Separate openai.modal.run and throughput.modal.run deployments also exist in configuration. Shared versus own deployment must be selected explicitly. For an OpenRouter offer, use a fixed Modal-only origin and do not fall back to OpenRouter itself; a successful fallback does not qualify Modal. Do not silently change production Basho routing during qualification.\n\n## Cost basis\nModal official GLM-5.3-Flash library on 2026-09-08 lists Shared rates USD 0.45/M input, 0.09/M cached input and 1.50/M output. These are published list prices, not the workspace's verified contracted bill. Shared uses token billing and workspace/model concurrency limits; dedicated hosting uses compute billing. No cached discount is assumed without distinct verified counts. The b70 proposal of 0.10/0.40 cannot be reused: at the Shared list rates it would lose 0.35/M input and 1.10/M output before gateway and other costs.\nIllustrative commercial worksheet, not an activated tariff: at 0.60/M input and 2.00/M output, gross contribution before overhead is 0.15 and 0.50 respectively (25% of revenue). A 1M input + 1M output workload costs 1.95 and yields 0.65 before fees at that proposal. Do not call it cheapest or profitable until actual invoices, failures, cancellations, edge fees, tax treatment and partner terms are reconciled. Dedicated deployment economics require actual GPU/CPU/memory/idle charges instead of these token rates.\n\n## Partner accounting\nExisting record-basho-vendor-spend! uses one shared configured price across upstreams, records streaming reservation as settlement, generates its own random job id, and has no upstream request id. It catches ledger-write failures. Consequently it is a budget estimate, not a partner invoice ledger. Read-then-append budget admission is not atomic. None of these properties is fixed merely by calling Modal the backend.\nAdded offline command: nbb scripts/api-smoke.cljs --partner-reconcile <normalized-export.json>. Input: records in vendor-spend shape, upstream-request-id per attempt, rates-usd-micros-per-million, rate-source, and invoice {provider, source, usd-micros}. It compares integer micro-dollar costs and rejects reservations, absent usage, missing upstream attribution, duplicate attempt ids, non-Modal attempts, absent evidence and invoice differences. Output is a reconciliation aid only: partner-billing-ready is always false; no charge or invoice is created. Evidence inputs are not independently authenticated, and matching amounts cannot establish customer authorization or idempotent charging. Per-attempt rounding differences must be investigated, not tolerated away. Synthetic test rates are not production price proof.\nRequired production ledger: stable partner request id and unique vendor attempt id; partner credential scope; origin/model and immutable rate version; terminal state (completed/failed/cancelled/unknown); actual usage or unresolved marker; separate customer settlement and all vendor costs. Cancelled/unknown usage is not zero. Retry charges and replay idempotency require explicit tests before real invoicing.\n\n## Data handling\nLocal source verification passed 4/4: synchronous run records omit message content, the operational ring holds 200 records, deferred jobs expire after 7 days, and deferred inputs cap at 200000 characters. This is a source-level disclosure check, not proof of physical erasure from every storage history or backup. A 200-record ring is not a time-based deletion guarantee.\nModal documents no payload storage for inference endpoints; Functions inputs/outputs can remain up to 7 days. Application logs have plan-dependent retention; volumes persist until deleted. Shared ingress is us-west, which does not prove all compute is US-only. Murakumo's edge, persistent operational records, async inputs and downstreams still need their own retention inventory. No whole-service ZDR claim. Before publication resolve Cloudflare logging, R2/Merkle history and compaction, account plan, actual endpoint type and deletion verification. The final notice must distinguish content from usage/accounting metadata, scope asynchronous storage separately, and list actual subprocessors.\n\n## Bounded execution specification\nAfter endpoint and credentials are verified: fixed Modal-only route; 20 minutes at one request per 10 seconds, alternating stream/nonstream, max64 output, no more than 120 requests; abort after 3 consecutive errors or USD1 attributable usage. This budget applies only to Shared token pricing, not dedicated startup compute. Record actual useful output, usage, status, first-content TTFT, total duration, upstream request id and no content. Do not infer capacity from low-volume success.\nCancellation: one stream cancelled after first content and one during prefill; compare vendor attempt terminal state and post-cancel billed tokens, then a fresh request. Client socket closure alone does not prove stopped compute. Recovery: inject 429/503/disconnect in an isolated adapter to verify Retry-After, bounded retry and no OpenRouter fallback; verify subsequent real Modal-only success. Do not restart a managed Shared pool or claim a mocked failure proves vendor restart recovery. Saturation limits must come from workspace endpoint metadata; no blind flood against shared capacity.\nThis execution has NOT run against Modal: the dashboard currently requires login and the two Modal serving modes are not yet disambiguated. Do not count b70's 7-day ledger or its overload test toward Modal qualification.\n\n## Contract\nModal's May 2026 standard terms include a restriction on resale and third-party direct access. Whether Murakumo's own authenticated inference API and OpenRouter resale are permitted needs the applicable Service Order or written vendor clarification; do not infer permission or categorical prohibition for every hosted application. Contracting entity and filing/address remain unverified. The website name Kotoba Labs Inc is not a registration document; do not reuse an older LLC identity. A concrete clarification draft is stored beside this record, not sent. No terms accepted, account changed or partner invoices issued.\n\nSources checked 2026-09-08:\nhttps://modal.com/library/zai/glm-5-3-flash\nhttps://modal.com/docs/guide/shared-endpoints\nhttps://modal.com/docs/guide/security\nhttps://modal.com/docs/guide/data-residency\nhttps://modal.com/docs/guide/billing\nhttps://modal.com/legal/terms\n"}]
